"""Independent complete own-field readings, written before candidate inspection."""
NOTES = {
('bukhari:2313','hi'): 'Read the entire383-character Hindi field. Its abbreviated chain introduces Umar’s written charity rules: the administrator may eat/feed a friend without accumulating money; then Abdullah ibn Umar administers his father’s charity and sends gifts to the Makkans where he stays. These are Umar’s document and Ibn Umar’s actual practice, not Prophetic speech. Both finite parts are supplied; the Hindi chain must not be filled from Urdu. No non-honorific explanatory parentheses or compiler tail are present.',
('bukhari:2313','ur'): 'Read the complete424-character Urdu field, including its longer Qutaybah/Sufyan/Amr chain. Umar’s actual written conditions and Abdullah ibn Umar’s subsequent administration/gifts are distinct attributed voices/actions in the supplied numbered report. Preserve the no-money-accumulation restriction and the Makkah residence relationship; neither statement is made Prophetic. No lexical or appended apparatus needs selecting as narration.',
('bukhari:2316','hi'): 'Read all398 Hindi characters. Uqbah reports Nuayman OR Ibn Nuayman being brought after drinking, the Messenger’s direction to the people in the house, and the reporter’s own participation using shoes/sticks. The source leaves the punished man’s identity alternative unresolved. Preserve the damaged opening chain and distinguish Uqbah’s first-person action from the Messenger’s instruction. This is a historical report, not a current instruction to the reader.',
('bukhari:2316','ur'): 'Read all441 Urdu characters. The full source chain leads to Uqbah; the Nuayman/Ibn-Nuayman alternative, house setting, Messenger’s order and narrator’s actual participation are explicit. Both the directive and first-person collective action remain with their supplied voices; no identity choice or omitted source wording is supplied. The whole field contains the report and its framing.',
('bukhari:2317','hi'): 'Read all670 Hindi characters. Aisha makes the garlands, the Prophet places them himself, and sends the animals with her father; do not replace the unnamed father with an external name. The parenthetic Makkah/sacrifice purpose supplies actual destination and purpose; the sending-caused no-prohibition parenthesis limits the final assertion and should remain. The own source chain grammar and the finite sacrifice sentence remain exact.',
('bukhari:2317','ur'): 'Read all605 Urdu characters. Aisha’s own garland testimony, the Messenger’s actions, her father’s role and the sacrifice are supplied. Retain both the Makkah-purpose qualification and the causal phrase that restricts which event imposed no prohibition. Treat the concluding claim as Aisha’s transmitted account, without adding any ritual inference or external father identification.',
('bukhari:2325','hi'): 'Read all478 characters in the Hindi-labelled field, including the Latin-transliterated opening chain and Devanagari body. Its mixed scripts remain source data. The Ansar propose dividing gardens; the Prophet refuses; the Ansar then offer work/fruit sharing and the respondents accept. Retain the Muhajir brother relationship and the parenthetic Muhajirin addressee, which supply actual participant scope. Do not present the Ansar’s later proposal as the Prophet’s own words.',
('bukhari:2325','ur'): 'Read the entire434-character Urdu field. The named Ansar address the Prophet, then address the Muhajirin after his refusal. Both parenthetic migrant-role expressions identify the actual counterpart/relationship rather than an interchangeable synonym. Preserve the acceptance and shared-fruit arrangement as the parties’ supplied dialogue, with the Prophet’s refusal distinguished.',
('bukhari:2327','hi'): 'Read all646 Hindi characters. Rafi describes Medina landholding and a specified part-of-field produce bargain, crop-loss alternatives, a passive prohibition, and the then-absence of gold/silver rent. The produce parenthesis supplies what the owner takes and should remain. The actor issuing the prohibition is not named locally, so no Prophetic identity may be imported. All conditional crop descriptions and the historical qualification are actual supplied report content.',
('bukhari:2327','ur'): 'Read all621 Urdu characters. Rafi’s own land/rent practice and alternating crop outcomes are supplied; the owner’s object is the specified part’s produce, so that parenthesis is substantive. Preserve the passive voice of being forbidden and do not infer its issuer from a sibling or parallel entry. The final gold/silver custom statement is actual narrator testimony, not a present legal conclusion.',
('bukhari:2329','hi'): 'Read all274 Hindi characters. Ibn Umar expressly reports the Prophet’s Khaybar arrangement for half the fruit and grain yield with its residents. Keep the exact source chain and both categories/quantity. No gloss, commentary, external identification or repair is needed.',
('bukhari:2329','ur'): 'Read the complete252-character Urdu field. Its Ibn Umar report explicitly attributes the half-fruit-and-grain agreement to the Prophet and identifies the local residents. The whole field is supplied framing and finite report; preserve its own wording without merging a parallel occurrence.',
('bukhari:2332','hi'): 'Read all486 Hindi characters. Rafi’s own field-allocation custom, differing harvest outcomes and explicit Prophetic prohibition are complete, despite the shortened Hindi chain and wording defects. The prohibition remains tied to the described arrangement, not generalized into an unqualified ban on all rent.',
('bukhari:2332','ur'): 'Read all525 Urdu characters, including the longer chain. Rafi explains the competing field shares and possible losses, then explicitly names the Prophet as prohibiting that transaction pattern. All qualifiers and the own source’s explanation within the report remain; no external contract definition is inserted.',
('bukhari:2334','hi'): 'Read all474 Hindi characters. Umar’s conditional policy about later Muslims and conquered cities cites the Prophet’s actual Khaybar distribution. This is Umar’s saying with a reported precedent, not an independent Prophetic instruction about all cities. The trailing hadith-related-chapters label and heading are source apparatus and should remain context; preserve the actual source ellipsis before that label.',
('bukhari:2334','ur'): 'Read all553 Urdu characters, including the visibly repeated complete chain. Preserve that source duplication rather than silently repairing it. Umar’s conditional saying and the cited Khaybar action are supplied; the final related-chapters heading is apparatus. Distinguish Umar’s policy reasoning from the Prophet’s cited historical action.',
('bukhari:2345','hi'): 'Read all512 Hindi characters. Ibn Umar knew sharecropping during the Prophet’s time; the ensuing report says he feared an unknown later instruction and stopped. Preserve that possibility as uncertainty, not an actual new Prophetic prohibition. The parenthetic precaution label repeats the already stated fear/causal sequence and can stay explanatory context; the finite fear and cessation remain selected.',
('bukhari:2345','ur'): 'Read all469 Urdu characters. The source’s Ibn Umar knowledge, later fear of an instruction he may not have heard, and stopping the practice are actual testimony. Keep the uncertainty and avoid asserting a new rule. The bracketed precaution gloss is redundant interpretive labeling alongside the already complete causal account, so context is appropriate without deleting the event.',
('bukhari:2346','hi'): 'Read all572 Hindi characters. The opening is damaged/abridged and does not supply the two-uncles introduction found elsewhere. It still gives the riverbank/allotted-area crop condition and explicit Prophetic ban, followed by Hanzalah’s question to Rafi and Rafi’s permission for dinar/dirham rent. Keep those voices distinct. The final Layth analytical statement about lawful/unlawful judgment is appended legal explanation, so it should remain context rather than a newly admitted report.',
('bukhari:2346','ur'): 'Read all661 Urdu characters. Rafi’s two uncles explicitly supply the initial historical practice; the Prophet bans the stated arrangement. Hanzalah’s question and Rafi’s coin-rent answer are separately attributed finite dialogue. The final Layth legal reasoning, including risk/gharar, is side explanation and remains context. Do not label Rafi’s permission as Prophetic speech.',
('bukhari:2347','hi'): 'Read this entire572-character own field, even though its words repeat2346. Preserve the separate provider occurrence and damaged initial attribution. The finite prohibited crop arrangement and actual Hanzalah/Rafi question-answer remain; Layth’s final legal appraisal is explanation context. No source identity merge is justified by exact textual equality.',
('bukhari:2347','ur'): 'Read all661 Urdu characters in this separate occurrence. The uncles’ transmitted practice, explicit Prophetic ban and Hanzalah/Rafi coin-rent dialogue are supplied under their actual voices. The appended Layth analysis and its gharar explanation stay context. Exact similarity with2346 does not alter either occurrence’s source identity.',
('bukhari:2349','hi'): 'Read all669 Hindi characters. Sahl’s Friday food account includes the old woman’s beetroot/barley preparation and later lunch/rest. Abu Hazim’s named I-do-not-know qualification about Sahl’s fat/grease wording materially qualifies that report and should remain as a separate reporter voice within the same family. The short this-fact parenthesis is redundant deictic explanation; the surrounding complete reason for happiness remains.',
('bukhari:2349','ur'): 'Read all646 Urdu characters. The meal narrative is Sahl’s, while Abu Hazim expressly voices uncertainty about the fat/grease phrase. Preserve that uncertainty instead of implying a settled ingredient statement, without making it Prophetic or a new independent occurrence. The bracketed this-fact phrase is redundant; keep the supplied Friday prayer/meal/rest sequence.',
('bukhari:2351','hi'): 'Read all603 Hindi characters. Sahl reports the milk/water cup, boy on the right, elders on the left, the Prophet’s permission question, the boy’s refusal and the cup given to him. Preserve the Hindi defects including the printed elders/leftover wording. The unnamed boy is not externally identified, and his personal answer is not Prophetic speech.',
('bukhari:2351','ur'): 'Read all626 Urdu characters. The actual seating and permission dialogue explain the reported cup transfer. Keep the boy’s first-person reply and the Prophet’s action under their own roles, with no outside name supplied for the boy. No compiler or lexical tail is present.',
('bukhari:2352','hi'): 'Read all927 Hindi characters. Anas supplies the goat/milk/well setting, the Prophet drinking, Abu Bakr left and the Bedouin right; Umar’s wish/request is distinct from the Prophet’s actual gift and right-side instruction. Preserve all source details, repetitions and the final successive-right condition; no statement by Umar becomes Prophetic.',
('bukhari:2352','ur'): 'Read all845 Urdu characters. The complete Anas scene identifies the household milk/water, seating and Umar’s concern, then the Prophet’s contrary recipient choice and instruction. Parentheses here are honorifics; keep the actual narrative and quoted voices. The Bedouin remains unnamed and no other edition supplies identity or wording.',
('bukhari:2366','hi'): 'Read all625 Hindi characters. Sahl’s own report describes the cup, youngest boy on the right and older Companions on the left; the Prophet asks permission, the boy keeps his portion and receives it. Preserve this occurrence separately from similar cup reports and keep the boy’s personal reply distinguished. The entire field is finite narrative/dialogue.',
('bukhari:2366','ur'): 'Read all562 Urdu characters. All participant roles, relative age, permission question, boy’s response and final transfer are explicitly supplied. The boy stays unnamed; nothing is filled from2351 or another source. No explanatory appendix requires admission.',
('bukhari:2375','hi'): 'Read all1,639 Hindi characters, including its badly joined lyric/action and incomplete Ibn Jurayj question sequence. Ali’s two camels, sale/walima plan, Hamzah scene, complaint and the Prophet’s departure are supplied. Keep the planned Fatimah marriage relationship and the explicit Ibn Jurayj speaker parenthesis, but leave the idhkhir definition, redundant thought label and repeated Ansari-house identity as context. Do not repair the damaged singer verse, omitted question or actor links from Urdu. The insulting speech belongs to Hamzah; the final pre-prohibition chronology remains a qualified source narrative statement.',
('bukhari:2375','ur'): 'Read all1,857 Urdu characters in full. The source gives Ali’s planned sale/marriage meal, the singer’s verse and Hamzah’s actions, Ibn Jurayj’s question/Ibn Shihab’s answer, resumed Ali narrative, Hamzah’s insult and the pre-alcohol-prohibition chronology. Keep the actual impending-marriage relationship, the parenthetic advance imperative completing the singer’s line, and the explicit Ibn Jurayj voice transition. The grass definition, repeated thought framing and duplicate Ansari-house apposition are explanation context. All distinct reported voices stay within this source event family.',
('bukhari:2376','hi'): 'Read all563 Hindi characters. Anas reports the Prophet’s proposed Bahrain grants to the Ansar, their condition for Muhajir brothers, and the Prophet’s future preference/patience statement. Retain the parenthetic grant-recipient relationship, the others-over-you comparison object and the afterlife meeting qualifier: these supply actual participant or temporal scope, not mere synonyms. No unconditional land entitlement is inferred.',
('bukhari:2376','ur'): 'Read all522 Urdu characters. The Ansar’s conditional acceptance is distinguished from the Prophet’s instruction. All three parentheses supply the grant recipients, comparison recipients and eventual meeting setting; preserve those meaningful scopes alongside the full own narrative. The numbered occurrence remains independent of related reports.',
('bukhari:2380','hi'): 'Read all344 Hindi characters. Through Ibn Umar, Zayd ibn Thabit reports the Prophet’s ariya exception using an estimate in exchange for dry dates. Preserve the technical term as printed without adding its absent definition or extending the permission beyond the given transaction.',
('bukhari:2380','ur'): 'Read all300 Urdu characters. The chain explicitly reaches Zayd and the Prophetic permission has the estimate/dry-date condition. The whole supplied field is narrative framing and actual content; there is no added lexical explanation to admit.',
('bukhari:2381','hi'): 'Read all444 Hindi characters. Jabir reports the three named prohibited arrangements, the unripe-fruit prohibition, the printed coin-exchange clause and the araya exception. Preserve the source’s exact conjunctions and technical terms; do not normalize the potentially awkward exchange wording or supply missing definitions.',
('bukhari:2381','ur'): 'Read all393 Urdu characters. Jabir’s attribution and each supplied trade/fruit clause are explicit, ending with the araya exception. Keep the actual scope and syntax rather than importing a canonical legal paraphrase. All text belongs to the provided report and chain.',
('bukhari:2382','hi'): 'Read all435 Hindi characters. Abu Hurayrah’s reported permission has a less-than-five OR within-five quantity alternative; Dawud ibn Husayn is expressly named as uncertain. Retain both quantities and the named uncertainty as a qualified transmitter voice in the same family. The this-was-said parenthesis is formula apparatus, while the final prior-explanation-of-ariya parenthesis is a source pointer; both can remain context without settling the quantity.',
('bukhari:2382','ur'): 'Read all390 Urdu characters. The exact five-wasq alternatives and Dawud’s uncertainty must remain visible; no preferred quantity is inferred. The bracketed saying-formula and final pointer to an earlier detailed account are apparatus. Keep the finite permission and the named uncertainty distinguished from each other’s speaker role.',
('ibnmajah:2291','hi'): 'Read all279 Hindi characters. Jabir reports an unnamed man’s property/children complaint and the Messenger’s actual answer about him and his property. Preserve both speakers and the own context without turning the passage into present personal legal advice or inventing the questioner’s name. The full field is supplied content.',
('ibnmajah:2291','ur'): 'Read all230 Urdu characters. The anonymous questioner’s complaint is distinct from the Prophet’s reply, both expressly provided through Jabir. No explanatory parentheses or compiler tail occur. Retain the exact source wording and its local attribution.',
('ibnmajah:2298','hi'): 'Read all682 Hindi characters. Abu Bishr reports hearing Abbad’s famine/garden/grain account: beating and seized clothes, complaint, the Prophet’s hunger/ignorance rebuke, and return plus one OR half wasq. Preserve the source’s tribal/name forms, its own questions/actions and unresolved amount. No external repair or imposed numerical choice is warranted.',
('ibnmajah:2298','ur'): 'Read all665 Urdu characters. Abbad’s first-person testimony and the Prophet’s rebuke/order are explicit, including the two different reasons and quantity alternative. All source chain/identity wording remains as printed. This supplied episode contains no legal appendix that should be substituted for the actual narrative.',
('ibnmajah:2303','hi'): 'Read all864 Hindi characters. Abu Hurayrah’s journey/camel narrative contains the group’s approach, the Prophet’s livelihood comparison, the group’s answer/necessity question and the conditional consume-without-taking reply. Retain the parenthetic milking purpose and such-condition restriction, which supply action intent and the limited reply’s scope. Preserve the source’s malformed wording without broadening the permission.',
('ibnmajah:2303','ur'): 'Read all851 Urdu characters, including the malformed opening approach phrase. The actual milking purpose and needy-condition qualification are meaningful narrative/answer scope and remain. The Prophet’s comparison, the group’s question and his eat/drink-without-carrying answer have distinct voices. Only the final numeric footnote glyph is apparatus; no absent note is supplied.',
('ibnmajah:2305','hi'): 'Read all221 Hindi characters. Urwah Bariqi explicitly reports the Messenger’s statements about camels, goats and horses, with the horse-goodness duration until Resurrection. Preserve all categories and time scope as supplied, without adding an authenticity assessment.',
('ibnmajah:2305','ur'): 'Read all202 Urdu characters. The complete source attribution and three clauses are present, including the duration qualification. No compiler tail or ambiguous missing speaker requires reconstruction; the wording remains this occurrence’s own.',
('ibnmajah:2307','hi'): 'Read all260 Hindi characters. Abu Hurayrah’s field explicitly attributes the wealthy/goats and needy/chickens direction plus the severe town-destruction conditional to the Messenger. Preserve the exact printed claim and actor/condition without endorsement, invented grading or silent alteration. No source commentary occurs.',
('ibnmajah:2307','ur'): 'Read all231 Urdu characters. The source expressly provides the reported direction and conditional consequence. Admission records the source’s attribution, not a new authenticity verdict or present instruction. The entire wording is retained as supplied, with no inferred external identity.',
}
assert len(NOTES) == 50
